ces to the states instead of  creating a one-size-fits-all program in  Washington DC will have will have some  states that will go one direction some  states that will go another direction  but people will be able to see as we did  in Indiana but each kind of reforms that  will improve access to health insurance  coverage lower the cost of health  insurance and ultimately improve  people's lives but even with federalism  are you prepared to give States the  right to waive a pre-existing condition  clause the president's made it very  clear that we stand by his commitment to  protect pre-existing conditions but at  the end of the day that we just have a  very clear choice about a week ago  Bernie Sanders and a number of Democrats  in the Congress laid out their ultimate  vision for health care and that is  single-payer health care driven out of  Washington DC and what what Graham  Cassidy articulates is a completely  different direction moving power out of  Washington DC empowering states to do  what we did in the state of Indiana when  I was governor we actually introduced  health savings accounts into Medicaid we  got people to move from the emergency  room to primary care we had some  underprivileged Hoosiers through the  first time ever were able to choose  their own doctor and and have their own  fund to be able to pay those first  expenses out of so there's lots of  innovation that can happen as I said the  president I truly believe in federalism  and state-based innovation and reform  and the momentum that you see behind  this bill 15 different governor's coming  out in favor of the bill I think will  only continue to grow in the days ahead  your vice president your staff is  turning purple so I think you have to go  yeah you have another engagement thank  you very much you don't want purple  statement no you don't not healthy thank  you very much
 

